# Normalise line endings to LF in both the repository and the working copy.
#
# This matters more here than in a typical project. `core.autocrlf=true` is a
# common Windows Git setting (it is set on the machine this repo was created
# on), and it rewrites text files as CRLF on checkout. Two things in this
# project compare bytes rather than parsed content:
#
#   * tests/fixtures/openalex_sample.jsonl is regenerated by its own generator,
#     which writes LF. Under CRLF checkout the unmodified fixture would show as
#     modified on every run, and its recorded size would drift.
#   * the dataset build records SHA-256 hashes of its inputs and outputs so a
#     later run can prove it consumed the same data. A hash that changes with
#     the checkout platform makes that proof worthless.
#
# `text=auto` lets Git detect binary files; `eol=lf` pins the working-copy
# ending so a clone is byte-identical on Windows, Linux, and macOS.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Model artefacts and figures, for when training and evaluation land. Marked
# explicitly rather than relying on binary sniffing, because a corrupted model
# file fails in confusing ways far from the cause.
*.joblib  binary
*.npy     binary
*.npz     binary
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.pdf     binary
